"Regarding mathematical novelty, beyond providing a unified framework for these three regression tasks, our main contribution is to bound the approximation error terms (see Section “Approximation error”, pp. 15-16). Approximating f* on a finite-dimensional basis introduces a nonzero bias" --It is worth noting that the specific bound presented in Proposition 2.1​ is a standard result in the kernel regression literature, which has also been derived for general RKHSs, albeit in a different form

\sum_{\lambda>\lambda_{j}}f_{j}^{2}=\sum_{\lambda>\lambda_{j}}\lambda^{s}\lambda_{j}^{-s}f_{j}^{2}\leq \lambda^{2}\|f\|_{[\mathcal H]^{s}}^{2}

Therefore, this work appears to be more of a synthesis of several established results, with its main advancement being a reduction in computational cost. This represents a valuable engineering improvement rather than a theoretical contribution. Based on this practical advancement, I am inclined to raise my score from 2 to 4.
